A||||24||dennis_6||305 Fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||dennis_6||09-05-2001||11:03 PM||||I brought my 2.8 back from the dead atleast for a while.  So no 3.4 conversion for me.  I was just wondering what everyone thought of a 305 V8 with a TPI system in a Fiero?  I am kinda of thinking about this down the road.  I am pretty sure it would work with one of Archies v8 kits and would be the path less traveled. Any thoughts?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||09-05-2001||11:04 PM||||Unless you can get a 305 TPI really cheap, I'd go with a 350.  More power, install is going to be the same.<P>Just remembered, Elfiero here had a 305 TPI in his 87 GT.  Sold it a little bit ago.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">        <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed<P><FONT SIZE="1">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<BR><FONT COLOR="red">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||dennis_6||09-05-2001||11:10 PM||||I got an auto tranny and don't want to put to much torque on it but I want a V8.  Figure I can get a wrecked firebird or camaro with a TPI system for around $500 and use archies deluxe kit for around 2k.  Not bad for a v8 swap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||09-05-2001||11:28 PM||||There's no substitute for cubic inches.  Unless you can get a killer deal for a 305, go for the 350.  Just my $0.02.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000004||voyagerspe||09-05-2001||11:47 PM||||I think the 350 is the way to go.  Im finishing my 88 foumula v8 conversion, I was going to do the 305 tpi thing but after costing out the parts to rebuild it I went with the 350 tpi.<BR>Its the same amount of work and a little less money to go with a 350.<P>  ||||reg||7
Z||000005||dennis_6||09-06-2001||03:40 AM||||I am using a stock auto tranny that I would like to keep around for a while so I really don't want a 350 that has 250 min. horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jake_Dragon||09-06-2001||07:25 AM||||Put the 350 in and use less foot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||pherder||09-06-2001||10:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dennis_6:<BR><B>I got an auto tranny and don't want to put to much torque on it but I want a V8.  Figure I can get a wrecked firebird or camaro with a TPI system for around $500 and use archies deluxe kit for around 2k.  Not bad for a v8 swap.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is what I originally thought about doing to my 86 SE/auto before I put the newly rebuilt 3.2 in it with a 440T4 transaxle.<P>My Formula will be getting my 350 with a TPI setup on it so my 305 TPI engine w/TPI manifold, wiring harness, MAP, and ECU are now up for sale.<P>Mine is for sale for $700 ... picked up at my shop.<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>--<BR>Paul Herder<BR>88 Formula - Artero kit car doner and 350 TPI<BR>86 SE - 3.2 w/440T4 Transaxle<BR>95 GMC - 350 TBI - Factory "Custom"<BR>92 Mazda Protege - Stock<BR>55 Jones - First Edition WIFE- all mods, equipment,updates :D<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135||||reg||1
Z||000008||Archie||09-06-2001||10:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dennis_6:<BR><B>I am using a stock auto tranny that I would like to keep around for a while so I really don't want a 350 that has 250 min. horsepower.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As you know, the 305 takes all the same parts to install in a Fiero as the 350 (or any other SBC engine). IMHO, your reasoning for wanting to use a 305 is a good one. You can always upgrade to a 350 down the road, like when you decide to beef-up the transmission.<P>Like my Daddy used to say, "any V-8 is better than no V-8".<P>Ok, well he didn't say that but he should have.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||09-06-2001||11:26 AM||||Not that my opinion means more than Archie on this point but I have to disagree. The only reason to use a 305 is if it was totally FREE. Why put all the money, time, and effort into a Hot Rod Fiero if your worried about breaking it? Or, have to later go back ad change it out?<P>If you are going to buy a motor, buy a 350. If your worried about the transmission, put a block of wood under the gas pedal. Then, if you ever get the coins to buy the transmission of your dreams, the real hard part is already completed. I mean, what would you rather do, change a motor and transmission later or just a transmission?<P>SBC 305s make good boat anchors but little else,<P>------------------<BR>Roy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 09-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||dennis_6||09-06-2001||04:06 PM||||I took a test drive in Camaro IROC with a 305 and had over 200,000 miles on it.  The car was not exactly what I call slow. <BR>And who knows I might need a good boat anchor one day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||09-06-2001||05:20 PM||||Sorry dennis,<P>My intent was not to call the 305 slow or that the acceleration wouldn't make you smile. What I was trying to say is, for the same money, you can have a 350. Then, if you ever decided to make even more HP, hop-up parts are easier to find and cheaper to buy. There just isn't enough MPG difference <BR>between the two motors to make a big difference.<P>Of course, with all that said, it's your car. You have the right to do what ever you want. One other thing to consider, should you ever decide to sell it, which motor will bring the most money. Remember, your initial investment is about the same.<P>Just food for thought,<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||dennis_6||09-06-2001||05:54 PM||||I just want to do a cheap easy install that won't put to much strain on my tranny.  I can get a TPI setup with engine out of a camaro for about 500 dollars.  I would drive that till it drops and then maybe beef up my auto or switch to a five speed and put in a 350.  Right now my choices are a v8 caddy and a 305.  I prefer a 305.  If I really wanted the horsepower of a 350 I am sure I can find a nitrous kit.  A 305 with nitro would be kinda cool.  The 305 is the underdog and I kinda like it.||||reg||10
Z||000013||Patrick's Dad||09-06-2001||07:38 PM||||You could put a "5.0" badge on your Fiero, too! The 305 &lt;i&gt;is&lt;/i&gt; a 5 liter engine, whereas, the cars normally associated with this moniker are 302's. 302's are 4875 cc's, making them, in truth, a 4.9.<P>As you can see, I considered a 305 conversion, too. Instead, I'm building up a 3.4, as it is my first engine, and I save 25% of the labor (25% fewer cylinders).<P>As was said, it's your car. No one else's. everyone has their opinions (and entitled to them), but only yours count. Have fun, and keep us posted on the progress.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000014||dennis_6||09-06-2001||08:18 PM||||That is all down the road.  I just wanted to see what people thought.  I am going to enjoy my 2.8 for a while since I finaly got it running after sitting for year.  But that engines days are numbered and I refuse to put another 2.8 in the car.  I will definitely post pics when I start the 305 conversion.  I belive the Firebird formula had a 5.0 sticker.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>              ||||reg||10
Z||000015||mrfixit58||09-07-2001||07:34 AM||||The 305 SBC conversion will cost between 2 and 3 more than the 4.9 for about the same performance and a lot more weight.<P>Just more food for thought,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
