Z||000017||V8Dreamer||09-07-2001||04:51 PM||||What's wrong with caddy parts???  I'm sure there are a couple of caddy's out there that will roast some mid-80's F-bodies.....<BR>just MHO.....<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000018||artherd||09-07-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dennis_6:<BR><B>I don't want any stinkin caddy parts on my plastic car!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I DO!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/northstar1.jpg">  <P>Tell me you don't want that!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000019||Sootah||09-07-2001||07:32 PM||||Well, since we are on the subject of engine swaps (as we are quite frequently) lemme ask, just how hard are they to do? I know almost nothing about engines, but when I get my Fiero and the engine blows up on me I really don't want to have to buy another one or pay a shop an assload to do it for me.<P>What are all of the major difficulties involved?||||reg||1
Z||000020||dennis_6||09-09-2001||06:51 PM||||I have never liked anything cadilac has ever produced.  I would not put a Ford engine or a Honda engine in my Fiero.  The only thing that might change my mind is that new v12 Caddy engine. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||12
Z||000021||Butter||09-10-2001||03:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> As you know, the 305 takes all the same parts to install in a Fiero as the 350 (or any other SBC engine). IMHO, your reasoning for wanting to use a 305 is a good one. You can always upgrade to a 350 down the road, like when you decide to beef-up the transmission.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I did this on my first Fiero engine swap.  So I know it works.  My cousin had a 305 he give me to build.  Good deal I thought.  I got approx. 320 HP after a few modifications.  It wound up having a crack in the block so it didn't last long.  <P>Thanks NAPA for the screwin'.  I had asked NAPA to check the heads and block for any problems.  I guess they misunderstood and thought I said to make sure it had problems this way they could keep selling me parts.<P>Anyway I used a carburated engine and it was a lot of fun.  I now use a 283, the only other v8 I had and with some mods got approx. 250 HP which is still a lot of fun.  The 305 did get two different 3rd gears so Archie is giving sound advise about the transaxle as always.  TPI is probally a better way to go as lateral G's can impede the performance when cornering hard especially if a weighted vacuum secondaries are used.  I'd say go for it.  It's a step in the right direction to finding out just how much HP you want to deal with.  Besides I enjoyed the installation and the satisfaction of the v8 sound when I got it done.  I now have a 350 block just begging me to mod and put it in.  Just my 2 cents worth.  Wish you well with your decision.  I'm still having fun with mine.<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000022||dennis_6||09-11-2001||01:41 AM||||Butter<BR>Thanks for the info.  I am just going to leave the 305 stock so I will only be running 190-215 hp.  If I get tired of that I will beef up the tranny and then add nitrous so I would be running 290 - 315 hp.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000023||Mickey_Moose||09-11-2001||01:45 PM||||Caddy &gt;&gt; just passing along some info that was told to me some time ago from a Caddy trained tech. Don't waste your money on a Caddy, it is GM's 'experimental' car. It is the car that gets all the 'new' features on it to test them out and see how well they work...after all the people that can buy the cars can afford to fix them. Kind of makes sense to a point...which car had the first heads up display or the first onstar...or just about anything else that was 'innovative' at the times.<P>just my $0.02  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||mrfixit58||09-11-2001||02:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mickey_Moose:<BR><B>Caddy &gt;&gt; just passing along some info that was told to me some time ago from a Caddy trained tech. Don't waste your money on a Caddy, it is GM's 'experimental' car. It is the car that gets all the 'new' features on it to test them out and see how well they work...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, that may be "somewhat" true for some of the electronics and probably good advice for the first year of the 4.1 liter motor (early 80s). But, his statement is truly garbage concerning the 4.9 and for the Northstar. The Northstar is a VERY proven motor. It's been around now since 1993. <P>I almost bought a police auctioned 93 STS which was rumored to have a bad head gasket. I called the local Caddy Service shop and inquired about a full gasket set for the motor and the Service rep was surprised at my request. When I asked him why he was so suprised he told me that they rarely see a northstar with ANY mechanical problems come into the shop. He also told me that all the parts I had asked about would have to be special ordered. As it turned out, the guy who bought it simply put a new battery and 1/2 gallon of collant into the motor and it runs and drives great!<P>If we all listened to all the experts and their opinion, why would we be driving our FIEROs? After all, have you tried to take your car to a Pontiac dealer lately? What was their response? Most don't even want to touch them calling them Pontiac's "mistake".<P>Please, ingnore your so-called "expert". Better yet, steer clear of anything he works on because it sounds like he's got a real problem.<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||13
